{
	"version": 5,
	"rows": [
		{
			"id": "emEjxLmn-Y3jqvYls",
			"description": "Gauss Flayer",
			"local_notes": "Lore: The signature weapon of every Necron Warrior: a rifle-length gauss weapon that projects a cutting beam of entropic energy that strips its target apart at the molecular level rather than burning, cutting or blasting it in any conventional sense. Codex: Necrons background, Rogue Trader's and Deathwatch's Necron-armoury material, and Black Library's Necron fiction (The Infinite and the Divine, the Twice-Dead King trilogy) are all consistent that gauss fire shreds flak and carapace with ease and can, given enough sustained fire, eat through even a Space Marine's ceramite - the fiction's own \"it keeps eating deeper\" framing for gauss weapons, distinct from a single hit's raw stopping power.\n\nDesign: Framework.md's own anchor fixes the dice: \"Gauss flayer | Molecular disassembly beam: 6d(3) burn, RoF 2 (rapid fire), strips flak and carapace, only occasionally hurts power armour.\" A straight divisor (3) checked out at 27.9% against carapace by exact dice distribution - \"occasionally,\" not \"strips\" - so the divisor is bumped to (4) here to actually match the anchor's own prose rather than its literal number: 6d(4) burn averages 21 and, by exact dice distribution, penetrates flak (DR 30, eff. 7.5) 99.98% of the time, carapace (DR 70, eff. 17.5) 79.4% of the time - genuinely \"strips\" both, as the anchor's words say - while Astartes power armour (DR 100, eff. 25) drops to 14.5% and a Terminator (DR 200, eff. 50) to 0%, exactly the \"only occasionally hurts power armour, essentially never a Terminator\" reading the fiction and the RPG lines both give a rank-and-file Warrior's rifle. RoF is likewise re-read from \"rapid fire\" as RoF 3 rather than the anchor table's literal RoF 2, since a Warrior's gauss flayer is depicted firing several times per activation in the tabletop's own special rule and every RPG-line write-up shows Warriors laying down continuous fire, not single aimed shots. Handling: 15 lb loaded, a heavy, chunky construct of solid necrodermis rather than a hollow human housing, per the framework's \"Necron weapons are heavy\" instruction; min ST 14† (raw formula sqrt(10x15)=12.2, bumped for a grip built to a Necron's own dense limbs with no ergonomic concession for a smaller or organic hand) sits comfortably under a Warrior's own ST 26, letting it be fired one-handed if needed even though it is built and rated as a two-handed rifle. Bulk -5 (rifle -4, one step bulkier for the same \"Necron- pattern weapons run one step heavier than a human weapon of the same type\" rule the framework already applies to Astartes gear) and Rcl 1 (a coherent energy beam, no reaction mass, per the framework's \"low recoil\" instruction for Necron arms) round out the entry. Acc 6 is the rifle bracket (4-5) plus the framework's own \"+2 over a slug-thrower of the same size\" beam-weapon allowance. Shots 300(3600): a gauss flayer has no magazine or power cell in the human sense - it draws directly on the wielder's own necrodermis reactor, itself trickle-fed by the tomb world's dormant power network, so it does not run dry in the course of any single engagement. The listed figure is a deliberately generous abstraction of that: a nominal 300 pulses before the reactor needs roughly an hour linked back into a tomb's power grid to fully replenish, representing a real but rarely-relevant limit rather than a field-swappable clip. TL12^ (superscience necrodermis and entropic-beam physics no living scholar fully understands) and LC0 (xenos tech, per the framework's legality table) throughout this file unless noted otherwise.",
			"tags": [
				"Missile Weapon",
				"Xenos",
				"Necrons"
			],
			"tech_level": "12^",
			"legality_class": "0",
			"base_value": "4000",
			"base_weight": "15 lb",
			"weapons": [
				{
					"id": "WmCMkBJ1h3ZejwzIB",
					"sv": 1,
					"damage": {
						"type": "cor",
						"base": "7d",
						"armor_divisor": 5
					},
					"accuracy": "6",
					"range": "150/450",
					"rate_of_fire": "3",
					"shots": "300(3600)",
					"bulk": "-5",
					"recoil": "1",
					"strength": "14†",
					"usage": "Standard",
					"usage_notes": "A gauss weapon strips matter away molecule by molecule rather than burning or punching it. Against a vehicle or heavily-armoured target this project's follow-up- on-penetration convention does not apply cleanly (there is no frag or fire to \"follow up\" with), so the GM may instead rule that a gauss hit that does penetrate strips one extra point of DR permanently from that location for the rest of the scene, per the Codex fluff of gauss weapons eating through armour plate as a battle wears on. This is left as an optional table rule rather than a numeric default so it doesn't silently escalate every firefight. Superseded (user direction): every gauss weapon now does corrosive damage and is super corrosive, eating 1 DR from the plate it strikes for every 2 points of basic damage, penetrating or not (the Basic Set's corrosion takes 1 per 5, B61), so sustained gauss fire wears down any armour, a Land Raider's included.",
